CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the U-Net architecture used as the noise-prediction backbone in a diffusion model. The diagram is organized as a symmetric encoder-decoder structure: the encoder path (left branch) shows a sequence of orange-shaded three-dimensional feature map volumes that progressively downsample the input from 256×256×3 through 256×256×128, 128×128×128, 64×64×256, 32×32×256, 16×16×512, down to 8×8×512 at the bottleneck, while the decoder path (right branch) shows dark steel-blue feature map volumes that mirror the encoder at each spatial scale as the resolution is progressively restored. Four horizontal “Concatenate” arrows connect matching encoder and decoder blocks at four resolution levels, forming the characteristic skip connections of a U-Net. A small blue square node labeled “t” injects the diffusion timestep via a “time embedding” branch, with arrows feeding it into each processing block. Two block types are annotated in a legend at lower right: a plain “Res. block” (used at larger spatial scales) and a “Res. block + self-attention” (used at smaller spatial scales near the bottleneck). This figure demonstrates how a conditioned U-Net with residual blocks, skip connections, and time-step injection serves as the learned denoiser in a diffusion generative model.

PROJECTION TYPE: oblique
Call setCameraView({ projection: 'oblique', obliqueAngleDeg: 45, depthScale: 0.5, heightFraction }) after all geometry is added — a standard cabinet-oblique pose; the plan does not measure per-figure oblique angles. The scaffold implements oblique as a shear of the eye-space projection matrix, so the front face stays undistorted, depth edges leave at obliqueAngleDeg, and labels track the shear automatically. Build the TRUE 3D object — the shear is the camera's job. Do NOT shear geometry, do NOT wrap the figure in a sheared parent Object3D, and do NOT disable automatic matrix updates. The scaffold also provides the reveal interaction: dragging eases the shear out to expose the real 3D shape and a toggle button returns to the printed view — do not implement either yourself.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - rounded-corner legend box in the upper-left corner
  - legend black right-pointing arrow labeled 'Res. block'
  - legend pale blue right-pointing arrow labeled 'Res. block + self-attention'
  - left input image feature plane with noisy texture, labeled implicitly by nearby dimension '256×256×3'
  - encoder 256×256 residual block volume immediately after the input image
  - encoder 256×256 residual/self-attention-capable block volume labeled '256×256×128'
  - encoder 128×128 block volume labeled '128×128×128'
  - encoder 64×64 block volume labeled '64×64×256'
  - encoder 32×32 block volume labeled '32×32×256'
  - encoder 16×16 block volume labeled '16×16×512'
  - central bottleneck 8×8 block volume labeled '8×8×512'
  - decoder 8×8 block volume to the right of the bottleneck
  - decoder 16×16 block volume
  - decoder 32×32 block volume
  - decoder 64×64 block volume
  - decoder 128×128 block volume
  - decoder 256×256 block volume
  - final decoder 256×256 output block volume
  - right output image feature plane with noisy texture
  - small black right-pointing flow arrows between neighboring encoder, bottleneck, decoder, and output volumes
  - four long orange-brown skip-connection polylines from encoder to decoder with arrowheads
  - four text labels reading 'Concatenate' above the skip-connection lines
  - diagonal dimension labels above or beside the corresponding blocks: '256×256×3', '256×256×128', '128×128×128', '64×64×256', '32×32×256', '16×16×512', '8×8×512'
  - bottom-left circular timestep node labeled 't'
  - blue-gray horizontal time embedding baseline labeled 'time embedding'
  - blue-gray diagonal injection arrows from the time embedding baseline into every encoder, bottleneck, and decoder processing block
